Yes. The transferable capability is Frame Suspension: temporarily accepting a local rule-set strongly enough to think, feel, and act inside it without confusing it with permanent reality.

Stories do this naturally:

enter frame
→ accept local rules
→ simulate consequences
→ experience meaning
→ exit
→ retain what was useful

That can be repurposed far beyond fiction.

Athletes do it with visualization. Actors do it with roles. Engineers do it with hypothetical models. Therapists use versions of it in exposure, rehearsal, and reframing. Kids do it constantly in play. Strategists do it when they adopt an opponent’s perspective. Designers do it when they ask, “Suppose this constraint disappeared—what would become possible?”

The crucial feature is the return path.

Make-believe is powerful because you can temporarily suspend ordinary constraints, but healthy use preserves:

entry
local rules
awareness of the frame
ability to exit
reality check afterward

So I’d give it a Pantheon operator:

Make-Believe Engine

baseline reality
→ choose frame
→ instantiate alternate rules
→ inhabit seriously
→ observe what becomes possible
→ extract useful operator
→ return to baseline
→ test what survives

And that last step matters. If a fictional frame teaches you courage, creativity, empathy, or a better strategy, those can be brought back. If the frame requires false premises to work, leave those premises inside the game.

That gives you the power of suspension of disbelief without losing the distinction between useful simulation and literal truth.